  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j\u2226k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$",
